---
// Round-3 panel item: prominent scope-of-claim disclosure. States exactly
// what SPS does and does not measure, and gives the three-disclosure
// citation guidance reviewers asked for. Factual, no marketing.
const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL || "/";
const base = rawBase.endsWith("/") ? rawBase : `${rawBase}/`;
---

<section class="w-full max-w-4xl mx-auto py-12 scroll-mt-24" id="scope-of-claim">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-2">
    What SPS does — and does not — measure
  </h2>
  <div
    class="rounded-lg border border-primary/30 bg-primary/5 dark:border-primary-light/30 dark:bg-primary-light/5 p-6 max-w-3xl"
  >
    <p class="text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-4">
      The Survey Parity Score measures <strong>distributional fidelity</strong>:
      how closely a synthetic respondent system's aggregate response
      distributions match the human response distributions recorded in these
      specific surveys — the Pew American Trends Panel (OpinionsQA), SubPOP,
      GlobalOpinionQA, and the General Social Survey. That is the entire
      claim. SPS does <strong>not</strong> measure the validity of synthetic
      respondents as substitutes for human research participants, the
      accuracy of any individual synthetic response, or the ability to
      predict real-world behavior, purchasing decisions, or future opinion. A
      high SPS means the system reproduces how surveyed populations answered
      these questionnaires; it does not mean the system understands people or
      that its outputs generalize beyond the measured surveys' populations,
      topics, and time windows.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-2">
      <strong class="text-text dark:text-text-dark">If you cite SynthBench results, please carry three disclosures with the number:</strong>
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 text-sm text-muted space-y-1">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-text dark:text-text-dark">Fidelity, not validity</strong> — describe SPS as distributional
        fidelity to recorded survey responses, not as accuracy, correctness,
        or human-equivalence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-text dark:text-text-dark">Dataset scope</strong> — name the datasets the score was measured
        on (Pew ATP / SubPOP / GlobalOpinionQA / GSS); a score on these
        surveys is not a score on your survey.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-text dark:text-text-dark">Operator disclosure</strong> — note that the benchmark operator
        also ships a scored product, and link the
        <a href={`${base}methodology/#operator-disclosure`} class="text-primary hover:underline">operator disclosure</a>
        so readers can weigh the conflict of interest themselves.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>
